  <h2>What is All-In-One Exercise?</h2>

  <ArticleImage src="/assets/images/articles/workout-routine.svg" alt="Illustration of a full body workout routine combining strength and cardio." />
  <p>All-in-one exercise refers to movements that engage multiple muscle groups simultaneously, providing strength, cardio, and flexibility benefits in a single exercise. Instead of isolating individual muscles, these compound movements work your entire body, making them time-efficient and highly functional.</p>
  <p>These exercises have grown popular because they deliver maximum results with minimal time investment. Whether you are a busy professional, a parent, or someone new to fitness, all-in-one exercises let you train effectively without complex routines or expensive equipment.</p>

  <div class="not-prose">
    <div class="card my-6 bg-brand/5 p-5">
      <strong class="text-ink">Quick Summary:</strong>
      <ul class="mt-2 text-sm text-body">
        <li>No equipment required for bodyweight all-in-one exercises</li>
        <li>Each exercise works 3-6 major muscle groups</li>
        <li>15-20 minutes delivers a complete full-body workout</li>
        <li>Beginner modifications available for every movement</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Benefits of All-In-One Exercises</h2>

  <h3>Weight Loss</h3>
  <p>Full-body exercises elevate your heart rate while building muscle, creating the ideal environment for fat loss. The combination of strength and cardio demands more energy, helping you burn more calories during and after your workout.</p>

  <h3>Muscle Building</h3>
  <p>Compound movements recruit fast-twitch muscle fibers and trigger growth hormone release. While not optimal for maximum hypertrophy, they efficiently build lean muscle across your entire body.</p>

  <h3>Cardiovascular Health</h3>
  <p>Continuous movement keeps your heart rate elevated, improving circulation, lung capacity, and overall cardiovascular fitness. Many all-in-one exercises are inherently aerobic.</p>

  <h3>Mobility and Flexibility</h3>
  <p>Routine movements through full ranges of motion maintain joint health, prevent stiffness, and improve your body's ability to move freely throughout daily activities.</p>

  <h3>Mental Health</h3>
  <p>Physical activity releases endorphins, reduces stress, and improves mood. The simplicity of all-in-one exercises makes them accessible for consistent mental health benefits.</p>

  <h2>Best All In One Exercises</h2>

  <h3>Burpees</h3>
  <p><strong>Muscles worked:</strong> Chest, shoulders, triceps, quads, glutes, core, calves</p>
  <p><strong>Difficulty level:</strong> Intermediate to Advanced</p>
  <p><strong>Calories burned:</strong> 10-15 per minute for a 150-pound person</p>
  <p><strong>Common mistakes:</strong> Rounding the lower back during push-up phase, not jumping at the top, poor landing mechanics</p>
  <p><strong>Beginner version:</strong> Step back instead of jump back, step forward instead of jump forward</p>
  <p><strong>Advanced version:</strong> Add a tuck jump, push-up clap, or dumbbell thrusters</p>

  <h3>Push-Ups</h3>
  <p><strong>Muscles worked:</strong> Chest, shoulders, triceps, core, quads</p>
  <p><strong>Difficulty level:</strong> Beginner to Advanced</p>
  <p><strong>Calories burned:</strong> 6-8 per minute for a 150-pound person</p>
  <p><strong>Common mistakes:</strong> Sagging hips, flaring elbows, incomplete range of motion</p>
  <p><strong>Beginner version:</strong> Knee push-ups, incline push-ups on a wall or bench</p>
  <p><strong>Advanced version:</strong> Decline push-ups, diamond push-ups, handstand push-ups</p>

  <h3>Squats</h3>
  <p><strong>Muscles worked:</strong> Quads, glutes, hamstrings, calves, core</p>
  <p><strong>Difficulty level:</strong> Beginner to Advanced</p>
  <p><strong>Calories burned:</strong> 8-10 per minute for a 150-pound person</p>
  <p><strong>Common mistakes:</strong> Knees caving inward, leaning forward, not reaching parallel</p>
  <p><strong>Beginner version:</strong> Box squats, chair-assisted squats</p>
  <p><strong>Advanced version:</strong> Jump squats, pistol squats, barbell back squats</p>

  <h3>Lunges</h3>
  <p><strong>Muscles worked:</strong> Quads, glutes, hamstrings, calves</p>
  <p><strong>Difficulty level:</strong> Beginner to Advanced</p>
  <p><strong>Calories burned:</strong> 7-9 per minute for a 150-pound person</p>
  <p><strong>Common mistakes:</strong> Knee extending past toes, leaning torso forward, uneven step length</p>
  <p><strong>Beginner version:</strong> Static lunges, supported lunges holding a chair</p>
  <p><strong>Advanced version:</strong> Jumping lunges, walking lunges with twist, Bulgarian split squats</p>

  <h3>Mountain Climbers</h3>
  <p><strong>Muscles worked:</strong> Core, shoulders, chest, hip flexors, quads</p>
  <p><strong>Difficulty level:</strong> Intermediate</p>
  <p><strong>Calories burned:</strong> 9-12 per minute for a 150-pound person</p>
  <p><strong>Common mistakes:</strong> Hips bouncing, hands not under shoulders, rushing the movement</p>
  <p><strong>Beginner version:</strong> Slow mountain climbers, elevated feet on a bench</p>
  <p><strong>Advanced version:</strong> Cross-body mountain climbers, spiderman climbers</p>

  <h3>Plank</h3>
  <p><strong>Muscles worked:</strong> Core (abs, obliques, lower back), shoulders, chest, glutes</p>
  <p><strong>Difficulty level:</strong> Beginner to Advanced</p>
  <p><strong>Calories burned:</strong> 4-6 per minute for a 150-pound person</p>
  <p><strong>Common mistakes:</strong> Hips sagging, butt in the air, holding breath</p>
  <p><strong>Beginner version:</strong> Knee plank, incline plank on a wall</p>
  <p><strong>Advanced version:</strong> Side plank, plank jacks, plank to push-up</p>

  <h3>Jumping Jacks</h3>
  <p><strong>Muscles worked:</strong> Shoulders, calves, hip abductors, core</p>
  <p><strong>Difficulty level:</strong> Beginner</p>
  <p><strong>Calories burned:</strong> 8-10 per minute for a 150-pound person</p>
  <p><strong>Common mistakes:</strong> Shallow jumping, not fully extending arms, uneven landing</p>
  <p><strong>Beginner version:</strong> Step jacks, half jacks focusing on arms only</p>
  <p><strong>Advanced version:</strong> Star jumps, squased jacks, plank jacks</p>

  <h3>Bear Crawl</h3>
  <p><strong>Muscles worked:</strong> Shoulders, chest, core, quads, glutes, hamstrings</p>
  <p><strong>Difficulty level:</strong> Intermediate</p>
  <p><strong>Calories burned:</strong> 9-11 per minute for a 150-pound person</p>
  <p><strong>Common mistakes:</strong> Hips too high or too low, not maintaining tension</p>
  <p><strong>Beginner version:</strong> Bear crawl hold, stationary bear position</p>
  <p><strong>Advanced version:</strong> Bear crawl with push-up, lateral bear crawl</p>

  <h3>Dead Bug</h3>
  <p><strong>Muscles worked:</strong> Core, hip flexors, shoulders, lower back</p>
  <p><strong>Difficulty level:</strong> Beginner to Intermediate</p>
  <p><strong>Calories burned:</strong> 3-5 per minute for a 150-pound person</p>
  <p><strong>Common mistakes:</strong> Arching lower back, moving too fast, holding breath</p>
  <p><strong>Beginner version:</strong> Dead bug hold without limb movement</p>
  <p><strong>Advanced version:</strong> Dead bug with light weights, alternating arm/leg reach</p>

  <h3>Glute Bridge</h3>
  <p><strong>Muscles worked:</strong> Glutes, hamstrings, core, quads</p>
  <p><strong>Difficulty level:</strong> Beginner to Advanced</p>
  <p><strong>Calories burned:</strong> 5-7 per minute for a 150-pound person</p>
  <p><strong>Common mistakes:</strong> Overextending the lower back, not squeezing glutes, knees collapsing</p>
  <p><strong>Beginner version:</strong> Floor glute bridge, supported with hands on floor</p>
  <p><strong>Advanced version:</strong> Single-leg bridge, weighted bridge, hip thrusts</p>

